course: dictionary
lesson: 9475
title: Statistically: what numbers tell us
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word statistically means judging by numbers. Statistics are facts collected from many people or things, such as how many children like apples. When someone says «Statistically, we'll be tired tomorrow», they are not promising that you will be tired. They mean that most people in a similar case would probably be tired.
explain: Read the words around it. Celestine says it at a sleepover, and the other girls just want a pillow fight. The clue is that she is talking about a group, and she is making a likely guess, not a certain one. A good check is to swap in "most people would probably" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Statistically speaking is a useful phrase. It tells the listener that the claim comes from data. Flying is statistically safer than driving, which means that when lots of journeys are counted, fewer crashes happen on planes per trip. It does not promise that your own flight will be safe.
fact: Statistically means according to statistics, judging by numbers and data.
fact: «Statistically» tells you that a fact comes from data about many people or things.
fact: Statistics can show what is likely, but they cannot tell you what will happen to one person.
fact: «Statistically significant» is a science phrase: a result is very unlikely to have happened just by chance.
fact: «Statistically more likely» means it happens more often in the numbers, but not always.
exercise: «Statistically, we'll be tired tomorrow.» What does statistically tell you? | It is a promise that you will be tired | *It is a fact based on numbers about many people | It means you are very clever | It is a wish that everyone will be tired
exercise: «Flying is statistically safer than driving.» What does this mean? | Your next flight will definitely be safe | Planes are faster than cars | *When lots of trips are counted, fewer crashes happen on planes | Driving is always dangerous
exercise: Which phrase is a science phrase meaning a result is very unlikely to have happened just by chance? | *Statistically significant | Statistically speaking | Statistically more likely | Statistically tired
test: «Statistically more likely» means what? | *It happens more often in the numbers, but not always for one person | It is a secret | It never happens | It always happens to everyone
test: Celestine says «Statistically, we'll be tired tomorrow.» Which swap best checks the meaning? | Nobody is tired at all | *Most people would probably be tired | Everyone is tired for sure | Pillow fights are fun
exam: Mia says, «Statistically, I will win the race.» Why can she not be sure? | Numbers never change | *Statistics describe groups, so they cannot promise one result | Races are not fun | Statistics always come true for one person
